  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThe marine layer was not as deep this morning as high pressure\nover the Desert Southwest begins to squash the layer. Low clouds\nwill continue to cover areas mainly within 10 miles of the coast\neach night and morning through the weekend. Slightly warmer\nconditions will be felt today as the high over New Mexico slowly\nexpands. 850mb temps rise more significantly by Friday, so \ntemperatures will warm another 3-5 degrees for most areas. This \nwill bring temperatures near 105 degrees from the Inland Empire \ninto the metropolis of the High Desert, as well as highs in \n80s/90s for coastal valleys and mountains. \n\nFor the weekend, the area of high pressure peaks in strength on\nSaturday. This is when we can expect the hottest temperatures.\nTemperatures will be well into the 90s for valleys and mountains\nof Orange and San Diego Counties and over 115 degrees across the\nlower deserts. The high weakens slightly on Sunday, but\ntemperatures only knock down a degree or two. All mountains will \nremain hot as well. Please severely limit or postpone outdoor \nrecreation in these areas, since temperatures will be well into \nthe 70s and 80s by the mid-morning hours. For those without A/C, \nmake sure to go to air conditioned areas and drink plenty of \nwater. Warm and humid nights in the 60s/70s/80s will lead to less \nrelief from the heat as well.\n\nBy early next week, the high weakens subtly and shifts back to the\neast. This will bring a degree or two of cooling for inland areas\neach day, but warm temperatures will still remain. Temperatures \nwill stay near to slightly above normal by Wednesday of next week.\nThe high may go into a position that will bring in a subtle \nreturn of monsoonal moisture and greater humidity to the region,\nbut rain chances still seem slim.",
